question: In the preoperative risk assessment of elderly cardiac surgery patients, gait speed (GS) is considered a physiological indicator reflecting physical fitness and frailty. Please, under the following constraints (#1, #3, #7): 1) Discuss the impact on the performance of predicting early mortality and major complications when gait speed is added to the EuroSCORE II (ES-II) model; 2) Explain why gait speed did not significantly improve the predictive performance of ES-II for the aforementioned outcomes; 3) Compare and explain the reasons why gait speed might improve predictive performance in earlier versions of EuroSCORE (ES, not ES-II). constraints: { "1. Conditions Constraints/Assumptions (Assumptions)": "- The study subjects are elderly patients undergoing cardiac surgery (in the clinical context targeting the elderly, not strictly by age threshold);
- Early mortality and major complications are defined following the clinical criteria of common postoperative 30-day or in-hospital outcomes;
- Gait speed (GS) is used as a surrogate indicator of frailty and overall functional status, and adding it to the model does not change the coding and weights of the original ES-II variables;
- No recalibration or refitting of ES-II is performed, only examining the marginal impact on discrimination and calibration by "adding GS" within the existing ES-II framework;
- The discussion is based on mechanisms commonly agreed upon in clinical practice and literature consensus, rather than conclusions from a specific study sample.", "3. Conditions Constraints/Applicability Range (Applicability Range)": "- Applicable to preoperative risk prediction scenarios for the inpatient cardiac surgery population;
- Applicable to the qualitative assessment of the impact on discrimination (such as AUROC) and calibration (such as calibration slope/intercept, Brier score) when extending the baseline model ES-II (adding GS);
- The extrapolation of conclusions requires cautious interpretation based on measurement differences between institutions, sample composition differences, and healthcare system differences;
- Does not involve quantitative stratified conclusions for specific surgical subtypes (such as only coronary artery bypass or valve surgery alone), but rather a general explanation for the overall elderly cardiac surgery population.", "7. Terminology Constraints/Symbols & Constants Standardization (Symbols and constants standardization)": "- ES: earlier version of EuroSCORE; ES-II: EuroSCORE II; GS: gait speed; EM: early mortality; MC: major complications;
- Performance (Perf) may refer to common indicators such as discrimination (AUROC, range [0,1], dimensionless) and calibration (such as Brier score, range [0,1], dimensionless);
- Probability values are agreed to range from [0,1]; all terms are used consistently according to the above abbreviations and definitions." }
